---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/url';

const description =
  'How PNH India Foundation works: patient information, peer support, help with access to diagnosis and treatment, clinician awareness, and rare disease policy advocacy in India.';

const programmes = [
  {
    title: 'Information and education',
    summary:
      'Clear, accurate, plain-language information about PNH for patients and families.',
    detail: [
      'Most PNH information online is written either for clinicians or for a different healthcare system. We produce material that a person in India, newly diagnosed and frightened, can actually use.',
      'This includes explanations of the condition and its treatment, questions to take to appointments, and guidance on navigating the health system.',
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Peer support',
    summary:
      'Connecting patients and caregivers with others who understand the condition from the inside.',
    detail: [
      'PNH is rare enough that most patients have never met another person with it. That isolation is one of the heaviest parts of the diagnosis, and it is the part we can do the most about.',
      'We connect patients and caregivers with one another, in whatever form suits them — one-to-one introductions, group conversations, or simply someone to message.',
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Access to diagnosis and treatment',
    summary:
      'Practical help getting diagnosed, getting to the right specialist, and dealing with the cost of care.',
    detail: [
      'Getting a PNH diagnosis in India can take years — one Indian case series recorded a mean delay of 3.7 years from first symptoms — and getting treated can be harder still. We help patients find haematologists with PNH experience, understand what testing they should have had, and navigate the funding routes that do exist: state schemes, hospital charity funds and manufacturer assistance programmes.',
      'We also document where the system is failing patients, because that evidence is what makes advocacy credible. India has no PNH registry, so at present nobody can say how many patients there are, where they are, or how many are going untreated.',
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Clinician awareness',
    summary:
      'Helping doctors recognise PNH earlier, so patients are not lost in years of misdiagnosis.',
    detail: [
      'Unexplained haemolytic anaemia with a raised LDH and a negative Coombs test, an unexplained clot in an unusual site, or unexplained cytopenias should each prompt a PNH test. Many do not, simply because the condition never comes to mind. Because only about 30% of patients ever have the dark urine PNH is named after, the one memorable clue is usually absent.',
      'We work to put PNH on the differential — through awareness material for physicians, and by helping patients and clinicians find laboratories whose flow cytometry meets international high-sensitivity standards.',
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Policy and advocacy',
    summary:
      'Getting PNH recognised under India’s National Policy for Rare Diseases.',
    detail: [
      'The National Policy for Rare Diseases, 2021 provides up to ₹50 lakh per patient for treatment at a designated Centre of Excellence. It names 63 rare diseases, and PNH is not among them — so PNH patients cannot presently claim that support on the basis of their diagnosis.',
      'PNH fits the policy’s own criteria squarely: rare, with a definitive treatment, needed lifelong, at high cost. Eculizumab has been approved for use in India since January 2025, which makes the funding gap the binding constraint rather than the availability of the drug.',
      'Getting PNH added to that list is our central policy objective, and it is the change that would do most for patients in this country. Alongside it we work to make sure the patient perspective is represented wherever rare disease funding decisions are made.',
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base title="What We Do" description={description}>
  <PageHeader
    eyebrow="Our work"
    title="What we do"
    lede="PNH is rare enough that patients often end up better informed about it than the first doctors they meet. Our work is aimed squarely at closing that gap — in the clinic, in the household, and in policy."
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="programmes">
        {
          programmes.map((p) => (
            <article class="programme">
              <div class="programme-head">
                <h2>{p.title}</h2>
                <p class="programme-summary">{p.summary}</p>
              </div>
              <div class="programme-body">
                {p.detail.map((para) => (
                  <p>{para}</p>
                ))}
              </div>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <div class="prose" style="margin-top: 3rem;">
        <div class="todo-note">
          TODO (Foundation): once the organisation has an activity history, add a section
          here with concrete outcomes — patients supported, awareness sessions
          held, submissions made. Specific numbers with dates build far more
          trust than descriptions of intent, and funders will ask for them.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--brand-soft">
    <div class="container cta-band">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Any of this useful to you?</h2>
        <p>
          Whether you are a patient, a caregiver, a clinician, or someone who
          wants to help — we would like to hear from you.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="btn-row">
        <a href={url('/contact')} class="btn btn--primary">Get in touch</a>
        <a href={url('/support-us')} class="btn btn--secondary">Support our work</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .programmes {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0;
  }

  .programme {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.5rem 3rem;
    padding-block: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 2.5rem);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
  .programme:first-child { border-top: 0; padding-top: 0; }

  @media (min-width: 58rem) {
    .programme { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1.35fr; }
  }

  .programme h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.3rem, 1.15rem + 0.7vw, 1.65rem);
    margin-bottom: 0.3em;
  }

  .programme-summary {
    color: var(--brand);
    font-weight: 550;
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .programme-body > :last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  .cta-band {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    align-items: center;
  }
  @media (min-width: 55rem) {
    .cta-band { grid-template-columns: 1.6fr auto; }
    .cta-band .btn-row { margin-top: 0; }
  }
</style>
